写真関係 (On Photography)
 In 写真関係 (Shashin Kankei), Miyako Ishiuchi offers not simply a reflection on her artistic career but a sustained meditation on photography as an encounter with time, memory, and corporeal presence.
Her attention gravitates toward surfaces marked by use—skin, fabric, walls, personal effects—objects that bear the imprint of lived experience; she articulates this impulse not as nostalgia but as a tactile ethics. To photograph, she suggests, is to approach the fragile threshold where material fact and emotional resonance converge.
In an era saturated with instantaneous digital imagery, Shashin Kankei insists on duration. It asks what it means to remain with an image long enough for it to disclose its quiet complexity. Ishiuchi’s work does not offer spectacle. Instead, it offers attention—an ethics of sustained looking that resists erasure.
SIGNED;
Published in: 2016;
Condition: Very Good;
SOLD.

ひろしま (“Hiroshima”) 
In this volume, Ishiuchi focuses her lens on objects and clothing preserved as relics of the atomic bombing of Hiroshima — attire like floral-patterned dresses, polka-dot blouses, handmade suits, and broken eyeglasses once worn by individuals who lived through the tragedy. These items, collected and stored at the Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum, become the subjects of her portrait-like images.
The images are spare, direct, and devoid of explanatory captions. In doing so, Ishiuchi entrusts meaning not to historical narration but to visual encounter itself. A dress becomes more than fabric; it becomes a witness. Its textures and patterns — once chosen with care by its wearer — now stand in silent testimony, inviting the viewer to imagine the life that once animated it.
In Hiroshima, photography becomes not just a record of what was, but a space in which what was continues to be felt. 
SIGNED;
Published in: 2008;
Condition: Very Good;
SOLD.

The exhibition catalogue Dissonance (Fondazione Mudima, 2008) brings together six women artists—Atsuko Tanaka, Yayoi Kusama, Yoko Ono, Shigeko Kubota, Takako Saito, and Mieko Shiomi—whose practices span the postwar period to the early twenty-first century.
From 30 September to 25 December 2008, the exhibition aimed to reinterpret post-war avant-garde art through gendered, transnational, and intermedia perspectives. Rather than a single stylistic school, Dissonance articulated dissonance as a productive force — emphasizing how these artists’ practices disrupted conventional narratives of modernism and expanded conceptions of medium, body, and participation. The title reflects rupture and tension within artistic form and institutional histories.
Published in: 2008;
